{
  "image_name": "749b922697b09b14595e0b22e0aa7a3c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how charge is stored in electrochemical materials and how those mechanisms combine in a p-type semiconducting polymer. At the top left, a purely capacitive process is shown: during charging and discharging, ions in the electrolyte rearrange at the electrode surface, forming an electric double layer without a redox change in the solid. In contrast, the middle schematic shows a Faradaic process, where oxidation and reduction involve electron transfer and ionic compensation near the electrode.\n\nThe polymer example on the right links these ideas. In its undoped state, the organic semiconductor appears neutral, while in the doped state it contains positive electronic charges (holes) on the polymer backbone that are balanced by incoming anions from the electrolyte. This illustrates volumetric electrochemical doping rather than simple surface charging.\n\nThe lower plots show how these contributions appear in measurements. In cyclic voltammetry, an ideal capacitive response gives a rectangular current–voltage loop, while a Faradaic redox event gives oxidation and reduction peaks; together they produce a broadened, asymmetric response. In constant-current charge–discharge, the capacitive case is linear in voltage with time, the Faradaic case shows plateaus around a redox potential, and the combined polymer response is a curved, hybrid profile.",
